  "slug": "docking-dynamics-049f",
  "summary": "Four preprints test how much sequence-based models recover about TF motifs, binding cooperativity, and mutational processes across ChIP-seq, ENCODE, and PCAWG data.",
  "synthesis": "The items share a focus on how much structure can be inferred from sequence and mutation data using purpose-built models. For TF regulation, a MAP-Elites approach recovers HOCOMOCO motifs across five liver ChIP-seq datasets, while BPNet models trained on ~2,000 ENCODE profiles indicate that motif cooperativity contributes negligibly to genome-wide binding. For somatic mutation, MOTET jointly infers multimodal processes linking SBS, DBS, and ID signatures in 2,583 PCAWG genomes, and EAGLE-MUT uses DNA language models trained on sparse esophageal adenocarcinoma and melanoma substitution data to predict context-dependent mutation probabilities. The common thread is delimiting explanatory power, including a negative result on cooperativity. All four are preprints, and the summaries do not report head-to-head comparisons with established tools.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
